PUBLIC RESPONSE: D. Atrial septal defect

The auscultatory picture is a right sided systolic ejection murmur maximal at the left upper sternal border 2nd-3rd ICS, radiating toward the left clavicle/neck, augmented by inspiration with a palpable thrill. That is the classic pulmonic area ejection / flow murmur produced by increased flow across a normal pulmonary valve and accentuated by the increase in right ventricular stroke volume with inspiration.

In a left-to-right atrial shunt the right atrium and right ventricle are volume overloaded, pulmonary blood flow is increased and a pulmonary flow systolic ejection murmur is heard at the left upper sternal border, often with inspiratory augmentation and can be associated with a thrill when flow is large. Chronic volume overload gives RV hypertrophy on ECG and progressive exertional dyspnea, fatigue and presyncope from right heart strain; a history of faint childhood cyanosis is compatible with an intermittent right-to-left component before pulmonary resistance rises.

The ECG left axis deviation with signs of right ventricular hypertrophy is the key discriminator. Left axis deviation together with RVH and right atrial enlargement is classic for an ostium primum / AV canal type atrial septal defect, the ASD variant that most closely mimics a pulmonary outflow obstruction murmur. Secundum ASD gives the same pulmonary flow murmur and RVH, the left axis deviation points to primum.

The other listed lesions are inconsistent:
Ebstein anomaly → holosystolic tricuspid regurgitation murmur, not systolic ejection at LUSB;
PDA → continuous machinery murmur;
Mitral valve prolapse → mid/late systolic click ± late systolic murmur at apex;
HCM with obstruction → harsh systolic murmur at left lower sternal border that decreases with inspiration and increases with Valsalva;
Tricuspid stenosis → diastolic rumbling at right lower sternal border;
VSD → harsh holosystolic murmur at left lower sternal border.

In strict anatomical terms the harsh ejection murmur with thrill and radiation to the clavicle is the textbook murmur of valvular pulmonary stenosis, which is not listed. Pulmonary stenosis would be the ideal match and is the reason many colleagues vote H. None of these choices. Among the choices provided, the pulmonary flow/ejection murmur of an atrial septal defect, most characteristically ostium primum, is the intended mimic and best fit.

